  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Thursday)\nIssued at 1256 PM MDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nAfter a relatively quiet weekend, things take a turn to become\nmore active late on Sunday as a deep Pacific trough digs southward\nalong the West Coast and begins to tap into increasingly rich\nmoisture associated with Hurricane Polo and the remnants of\nOdalys. Increasing southwesterly flow aloft and large-scale \nascent will support a return of showers and thunderstorms Sunday \nevening, initially over western New Mexico and the Continental \nDivide before spreading eastward and becoming more widespread \nMonday and Tuesday as the trough closes off.\n\nWith the influence of this tropical moisture plume, PWATs are\nforecast to reach well above seasonal normals, with the NAEFS and\nENS Standard Anomalies indicating above normal PWATs across\nportions of the region by roughly 2-4 standard deviations. The\ncombination of anomalous moisture, instability, and increasing\nupper-level forcing should support periods of moderate to heavy\nrainfall. Storms may also become more organized as stronger flow\naloft overspreads the region, introducing a potential severe\nweather component in addition to the flash flood threat.\n\nFlash flooding is still our main concern at this time,\nparticularly across areas that will remain saturated from this\nweek's rainfall. Additional rainfall of 1-3 inches will be\npossible across portions of New Mexico, with locally higher\ntotals where storms train or repeatedly affect the same \nlocations. This could result in renewed flash flooding as well as\nrises on area streams, tributaries, and river. WPC has maintained\na Slight Risk for excessive rainfall and flash flooding across \nmuch of the area during the early part of next week.\n\nIt is worth mentioning that there is still uncertainty regarding \nthe exact evolution and track of the trough/low and tropical \nremnants, even though long-term deterministic, ensemble, and AI \nguidance have come into slightly better agreement with the most \nrecent 12Z runs. All that to say, the location and magnitude of \nthe heaviest rainfall may likely change with subsequent guidance.\nNonetheless, confidence is increasing in a wetter and more active\npattern beginning Sunday evening and continuing through at least \nTuesday, with conditions potentially remaining unsettled into \nmidweek.",
